Shame! Shame!
Shame! Shame!


00:32:34.275 --> 00:32:38.234
Shame! Shame! Shame! Shame!


00:32:42.116 --> 00:32:44.516
- I love you, Mike!


00:32:44.552 --> 00:32:45.917
I love you, Mike!


00:33:12.980 --> 00:33:14.641
- Welcome to the first
of three debates


00:33:14.682 --> 00:33:16.274
among the major candidates


00:33:16.317 --> 00:33:18.285
for president
of the United States,


00:33:18.319 --> 00:33:21.447
sponsored by the Commission
on Presidential Debates.


00:33:21.489 --> 00:33:24.617
- Mr. President, yesterday
tens of thousands of people


00:33:24.658 --> 00:33:26.626
paraded past the White House


00:33:26.660 --> 00:33:30.289
to demonstrate their concern
about the disease AIDS.


00:33:30.331 --> 00:33:32.799
A celebrated member
of your commission,


00:33:32.833 --> 00:33:34.460
Magic Johnson,


00:33:34.502 --> 00:33:38.461
quipped, saying that
there was too much inaction.


00:33:38.506 --> 00:33:40.974
Where is this widespread feeling
coming from


00:33:41.008 --> 00:33:44.136
that your administration
is not doing enough about AIDS?


00:33:44.178 --> 00:33:46.476
- I can't tell you
where it's coming from,


00:33:46.514 --> 00:33:49.312
but I am very much concerned
about AIDS,


00:33:49.350 --> 00:33:50.817
and I believe that we've got


00:33:50.851 --> 00:33:52.819
the best researchers
in the world


00:33:52.853 --> 00:33:55.151
out there at NIH
working the problem.


00:33:55.189 --> 00:33:56.656
It's one of the few diseases


00:33:56.690 --> 00:33:58.317
where behavior matters.


00:33:58.359 --> 00:33:59.986
And I once called on somebody,


00:34:00.027 --> 00:34:01.324
"Well, change your behavior."


00:34:01.362 --> 00:34:02.989
If the behavior you're using,


00:34:03.030 --> 00:34:04.998
uh, prone to cause AIDS,


00:34:05.032 --> 00:34:06.090
change the behavior.


00:34:06.133 --> 00:34:07.157
Next thing I know,


00:34:07.201 --> 00:34:09.499
one of these ACT UP groups
is out,


00:34:09.537 --> 00:34:11.505
saying, "Bush ought
to change his behavior."


00:34:11.539 --> 00:34:13.530
You can't talk about it
rationally.


00:34:32.560 --> 00:34:37.020
- Let everyone here know


00:34:37.064 --> 00:34:41.194
that this is not
a political funeral


00:34:41.235 --> 00:34:43.362
for Mark Fisher,


00:34:43.404 --> 00:34:47.033
who wouldn't let us burn


00:34:47.074 --> 00:34:50.532
or bury his courage


00:34:50.578 --> 00:34:52.842
or his love for us


00:34:52.880 --> 00:34:56.372
any more than he would let
the earth take his body


00:34:56.417 --> 00:35:00.376
until it was already
in flight.


00:35:00.421 --> 00:35:03.049
He asked for this ceremony


00:35:03.090 --> 00:35:05.217
not so we could bury him


00:35:05.259 --> 00:35:10.561
but so we could celebrate
his undying anger.


00:35:10.598 --> 00:35:13.897
This isn't a political funeral
for Mark;


00:35:13.934 --> 00:35:15.902
it's a political funeral


00:35:15.936 --> 00:35:20.396
for the man who killed him
and so many others


00:35:20.441 --> 00:35:23.740
and is slowly killing me,


00:35:23.777 --> 00:35:26.439
whose name curls my tongue


00:35:26.480 --> 00:35:28.846
and curdles my breath.


00:35:28.882 --> 00:35:34.081
George Bush, we believe
you'll be defeated tomorrow


00:35:34.121 --> 00:35:35.418
because we believe


00:35:35.456 --> 00:35:38.755
there's still some justice left
in the universe


00:35:38.792 --> 00:35:43.252
and some compassion left
in the American people.


00:35:43.297 --> 00:35:45.925
But whether or not you are,


00:35:45.966 --> 00:35:49.925
here and now,
standing by Mark's body,


00:35:49.970 --> 00:35:52.768
we put this curse on you.


00:35:52.806 --> 00:35:55.775
Mark's spirit will haunt you


00:35:55.809 --> 00:35:58.437
until the end of your days,


00:35:58.479 --> 00:36:02.108
so that in the moment
of your defeat,


00:36:02.149 --> 00:36:04.447
you'll remember our defeats.


00:36:04.485 --> 00:36:06.453
And in the moment
of your death,


00:36:06.487 --> 00:36:11.117
you'll remember our deaths.


00:36:11.158 --> 00:36:16.619
As for Mark, when the living
can no longer speak,


00:36:16.664 --> 00:36:20.031
the dead may speak for them.


00:36:20.067 --> 00:36:23.628
Mark's voice is here with us,


00:36:23.671 --> 00:36:26.139
as is the voice of Pericles,


00:36:26.173 --> 00:36:30.803
who, two millennia ago,
warned the Athenian soldiers


00:36:30.844 --> 00:36:32.812
who didn't have to die


00:36:32.846 --> 00:36:35.474
and in whose death
he was complicit


00:36:35.516 --> 00:36:38.314
but who had the nobility
to say


00:36:38.352 --> 00:36:42.482
that their memorial
was the whole Earth.


00:36:42.523 --> 00:36:45.981
Let the whole Earth
hear us now.


00:36:46.026 --> 00:36:49.826
We beg, we pray, we demand


00:36:49.863 --> 00:36:54.163
that this epidemic end!


00:36:54.201 --> 00:36:58.001
Not just so we may live


00:36:58.038 --> 00:37:00.506
but so that Mark's soul


00:37:00.541 --> 00:37:03.510
may rest in peace at last.


00:37:09.149 --> 00:37:12.175
In anger and in grief,


00:37:12.219 --> 00:37:15.882
this fight is not over
till all of us are safe.


00:37:23.397 --> 00:37:24.864
Act up.


00:37:24.898 --> 00:37:26.195
Fight back.


00:37:26.233 --> 00:37:29.532
Fight AIDS.


00:39:04.264 --> 00:39:05.663
- The debate over AIDS therapy


00:39:05.699 --> 00:39:08.133
has reignited
with a new European study


00:39:08.168 --> 00:39:11.467
that challenges
the effectiveness of AZT,


00:39:11.505 --> 00:39:14.633
the widely prescribed drug
used to treat the HIV virus.


00:39:14.675 --> 00:39:18.634
- This study disproves
what every other study proves,


00:39:18.679 --> 00:39:22.479
is that the drug is at best
modest, mostly useless,


00:39:22.516 --> 00:39:23.949
not good for you
in the beginning.


00:39:23.984 --> 00:39:27.010
- Two other drugs,
ddI and ddC,


00:39:27.054 --> 00:39:29.818
were approved because
they were as good as AZT,


00:39:29.857 --> 00:39:32.826
which means that they might not
be very useful either.


00:39:32.860 --> 00:39:35.328
All this
is profoundly dispiriting


00:39:35.362 --> 00:39:37.353
for advocates
of people with AIDS.


00:39:37.397 --> 00:39:39.991
- We thought we had made
some advance in AIDS treatment


00:39:40.033 --> 00:39:41.500
over the last five years,


00:39:41.535 --> 00:39:44.163
and these studies show
that we really haven't.


00:39:44.204 --> 00:39:46.172
- Many activists now admit


00:39:46.206 --> 00:39:48.106
their demands
were shortsighted.


00:39:48.141 --> 00:39:50.166
- It's been a huge expenditure,


00:39:50.210 --> 00:39:53.008
a waste of money
for the U.S. taxpayer,


00:39:53.046 --> 00:39:55.014
and it was a naiveté
on our part


00:39:55.048 --> 00:39:57.642
to think that the magic bullet
was out there.


00:39:57.684 --> 00:40:02.018
It just had to be tested
in humans and, uh...


00:40:02.055 --> 00:40:03.579
given to us as the cure.


00:40:03.624 --> 00:40:05.023
- Many doctors and scientists


00:40:05.058 --> 00:40:07.185
say the bleak results
presented here


00:40:07.227 --> 00:40:09.024
indicate the U.S. government


00:40:09.062 --> 00:40:11.030
needs to
substantially reorganize


00:40:11.064 --> 00:40:13.532
the way it conducts
AIDS research.


00:40:13.567 --> 00:40:16.092
Robert Bazell, NBC News,
Berlin.


00:40:22.109 --> 00:40:24.202
- There's been this big crisis
at St. Vincent's


00:40:24.244 --> 00:40:28.704
about the way that we talk about
the, uh--


00:40:28.749 --> 00:40:30.080
what happened in Berlin.


00:40:30.117 --> 00:40:31.550
Couple of doctors said
they were


00:40:31.585 --> 00:40:34.110
afraid of people
committing suicide now.


00:40:34.154 --> 00:40:38.386
- I don't feel like,
"Oh, now I just"--


00:40:38.425 --> 00:40:40.552
I don't feel,
"Oh, now I want to give up.


00:40:40.594 --> 00:40:43.722
Now I want to stop living
because AZT doesn't work."


00:40:43.764 --> 00:40:45.061
- I've felt forever now


00:40:45.098 --> 00:40:46.895
that I'm not gonna outlive
this epidemic,


00:40:46.934 --> 00:40:49.061
that I will--
that I will die from this.


00:40:49.102 --> 00:40:50.899
- You know,
maybe that is our future,


00:40:50.938 --> 00:40:52.565
that we're gonna
watch each other die.


00:40:52.606 --> 00:40:54.574
It's--that's not a new thought.


00:40:54.608 --> 00:40:57.941
We've been thinking that
ever since we started the group.


00:40:57.978 --> 00:41:01.573
- The way that the recent
spate of deaths is--


00:41:01.615 --> 00:41:02.741
I don't know.


00:41:02.783 --> 00:41:05.251
It all seems
so much more apocalyptic.


00:41:05.285 --> 00:41:09.244
Like, the story doesn't seem


00:41:09.289 --> 00:41:12.417
to have this relationship
to effective treatment


00:41:12.459 --> 00:41:13.756
or a cure anymore.


00:41:13.794 --> 00:41:15.921
It now seems to have
this relationship to death.


00:41:15.963 --> 00:41:17.931
It ends--
it ends with everybody dying.


00:41:17.965 --> 00:41:19.432
Will the last person alive
in Chelsea


00:41:19.466 --> 00:41:20.956
please turn out the lights?


00:41:30.644 --> 00:41:32.612
- Joining me now
is Dr. David Kessler,


00:41:32.646 --> 00:41:35.342
the commissioner of the
Food and Drug Administration.


00:41:35.382 --> 00:41:36.872
Dr. Kessler, how excited


00:41:36.917 --> 00:41:39.283
should we get about
this new family of drugs,


00:41:39.319 --> 00:41:40.946
these protease inhibitors?


00:41:40.988 --> 00:41:42.785
- I don't want to overpromise,


00:41:42.823 --> 00:41:45.291
but these are
the most potent drugs


00:41:45.325 --> 00:41:48.226
we've seen against the virus.


00:41:48.261 --> 00:41:49.353
- I know that
you are pushing


00:41:49.396 --> 00:41:51.728
for faster FDA approval
of these drugs.


00:41:51.765 --> 00:41:53.460
I know that the application
has just gone


00:41:53.500 --> 00:41:55.127
for the first of these drugs
to the FDA.


00:41:55.168 --> 00:41:56.533
It's still
gonna take six months.


00:41:56.570 --> 00:42:00.472
- We will turn around that
application as quick as ever.


00:42:00.507 --> 00:42:03.476
We're approving drugs in
a matter of months these days.


00:42:03.510 --> 00:42:05.808
- But you were telling me
that's six months, right?


00:42:05.846 --> 00:42:07.074
That's as quick as possible.


00:42:07.114 --> 00:42:08.911
- We may be able to do it
even a little quicker.


00:42:08.949 --> 00:42:10.314
- A split has developed


00:42:10.350 --> 00:42:12.147
between those
who want rapid approval


00:42:12.185 --> 00:42:14.813
based on early indicators
of success


00:42:14.855 --> 00:42:17.323
and the Treatment Action Group,
or TAG.


00:42:17.357 --> 00:42:19.484
TAG has asked the FDA
to reconsider


00:42:19.526 --> 00:42:22.495
the accelerated
approval process.


00:42:22.529 --> 00:42:24.497
- We told the FDA,
"No."


00:42:24.531 --> 00:42:27.659
"The company has asked you
to approve that drug too soon.


00:42:27.701 --> 00:42:30.329
They need a little bit
more data first."


00:42:30.370 --> 00:42:32.634
- TAG is asking the FDA
to take a closer look


00:42:32.672 --> 00:42:35.505
at Saquinavir,
the first protease inhibitor


00:42:35.542 --> 00:42:36.736
to seek approval.


00:42:36.777 --> 00:42:37.903
- We need to make sure


00:42:37.944 --> 00:42:39.673
we don't repeat
the mistake we made


00:42:39.713 --> 00:42:41.044
with AZT and ddI.


00:42:41.081 --> 00:42:42.844
This is a new class of drugs.


00:42:42.883 --> 00:42:45.477
We need to know if it works.


00:42:45.519 --> 00:42:48.579
The battle over
early approval of Saquinavir


00:42:48.622 --> 00:42:52.524
was a really pivotal moment
for treatment activism.


00:42:52.559 --> 00:42:56.825
And we took such shit for it,
from within.


00:42:56.863 --> 00:42:58.330
- There are a lot of people
who do agree


00:42:58.365 --> 00:43:00.094
that there are problems,
serious problems,


00:43:00.133 --> 00:43:03.102
the way drug testing
and drug trials are run.


00:43:03.136 --> 00:43:07.698
But there are many of us
who feel that halting


00:43:07.741 --> 00:43:11.040
accelerated approval
is not necessarily the answer.


00:43:11.078 --> 00:43:12.409
- TAG made their proposal


00:43:12.446 --> 00:43:16.041
for this large simple trial
of 18,000 people


00:43:16.083 --> 00:43:18.916
that they want to put
in a placebo control trial


00:43:18.952 --> 00:43:21.216
where 1/3 would get placebo.


00:43:21.254 --> 00:43:24.883
They pushed this idea on the FDA
at a secret meeting,


00:43:24.925 --> 00:43:27.052
which was not announced
to the community.


00:43:27.094 --> 00:43:30.222
This is something we have fought
hard and long for.


00:43:30.263 --> 00:43:33.323
We've been arrested to get
accelerated approval through.


00:43:33.366 --> 00:43:35.732
- It's the behavior
that I have a problem with.


00:43:35.769 --> 00:43:37.498
It's the work they're doing


00:43:37.537 --> 00:43:39.368
that I have a problem with,
and it's--


00:43:39.406 --> 00:43:40.896
this is what I am gonna fight.


00:43:40.941 --> 00:43:43.341
I'm not interested
in mud wrestling with the boys.


00:43:43.376 --> 00:43:45.742
I am absolutely enraged
that there are people


00:43:45.779 --> 00:43:49.078
who have appointed themselves
elitist representatives


00:43:49.116 --> 00:43:50.413
and represent themselves


00:43:50.450 --> 00:43:52.077
as the single voice
of this epidemic.


00:43:52.119 --> 00:43:53.416
I am gonna fight them,


00:43:53.453 --> 00:43:54.920
my patients
are gonna fight them,


00:43:54.955 --> 00:43:56.752
and you goddamn well
better fight them!


00:44:03.530 --> 00:44:05.259
- Apparently there was
a big discussion


00:44:05.298 --> 00:44:07.596
on accelerated approval
and protease drug development


00:44:07.634 --> 00:44:09.101
last week on the floor,


00:44:09.136 --> 00:44:12.594
so we just wanted to give you
sort of the TAG perspective


00:44:12.639 --> 00:44:14.607
and give you a sense
of our proposal


00:44:14.641 --> 00:44:16.040
around protease
drug development


00:44:16.076 --> 00:44:17.270
so we can start
from a baseline


00:44:17.310 --> 00:44:18.868
of common understanding
and knowledge.


00:44:18.912 --> 00:44:22.211
- This proposal is not about
taking expanded access away,


00:44:22.249 --> 00:44:24.114
taking accelerated approval
away.


00:44:24.151 --> 00:44:25.618
This is about
adding something.


00:44:25.652 --> 00:44:28.280
This is about figuring out
how do we get information


00:44:28.321 --> 00:44:29.948
about how to actually
use these drugs.


00:44:29.990 --> 00:44:33.357
- You need more people when
you have a less powerful drug.


00:44:33.393 --> 00:44:34.860
If we were dealing
with a penicillin,


00:44:34.895 --> 00:44:36.795
we could do it in 20 people.


00:44:36.830 --> 00:44:38.627
So we put together
a large simple trial


00:44:38.665 --> 00:44:40.963
that tried to synthesize
expanded access


00:44:41.001 --> 00:44:42.298
in large simple trials.


00:44:42.335 --> 00:44:45.634
And what we did is,
we presented it to Merck.


00:44:45.672 --> 00:44:47.299
We presented it to the FDA.


00:44:47.340 --> 00:44:49.638
We wanted to start
a community discussion.


00:44:49.676 --> 00:44:51.803
- I just wanted
to thank Gregg and Derek


00:44:51.845 --> 00:44:53.972
for coming
to tell us about this.


00:44:55.682 --> 00:44:57.809
Because we hadn't heard anything


00:44:57.851 --> 00:44:59.910
until we read about it
in Barron's.


00:44:59.953 --> 00:45:01.386
- If you wanted to hear
the proposal,


00:45:01.421 --> 00:45:02.820
you could have heard it
40 times.


00:45:02.856 --> 00:45:04.255
It's been talked about
all over.


00:45:04.291 --> 00:45:05.986
- Dr. Cotler wants to speak.


00:45:06.026 --> 00:45:08.494
- TAG is talking about
getting accurate information.


00:45:08.528 --> 00:45:11.326
ACT UP is talking about
making drugs available.


00:45:11.364 --> 00:45:13.161
There should be a way
to mesh those two.


00:45:13.200 --> 00:45:14.997
It's really not
one or the other.


00:45:15.035 --> 00:45:17.731
Your two groups are really
talking past one another.


00:45:17.771 --> 00:45:19.170
Try not to scream.


00:45:19.206 --> 00:45:22.004
Try not to go at each other's
throats, but just talk,


00:45:22.042 --> 00:45:24.909
because the differences
really can be bridged.


00:45:26.913 --> 00:45:27.902
- No, no, no.


00:45:27.948 --> 00:45:29.245
- Hey!
- Shh.


00:45:30.884 --> 00:45:33.011
- Quiet!


00:45:33.053 --> 00:45:36.511
- '93 to '95
were the worst years.


00:45:36.556 --> 00:45:40.185
It was
a really terrifying time.


00:45:40.227 --> 00:45:42.218
They were the worst years.


00:45:46.399 --> 00:45:48.867
And then we got lucky.


00:46:37.083 --> 00:46:39.244
- Um...


00:46:39.286 --> 00:46:44.087
You know, just losing...


00:46:44.124 --> 00:46:47.582
And, uh...


00:46:47.627 --> 00:46:51.085
just so many...


00:46:51.131 --> 00:46:52.928
so many good people.


00:46:52.966 --> 00:46:56.595
And...
uh...


00:46:56.636 --> 00:47:00.595
You know, like any war,


00:47:00.640 --> 00:47:03.473
you wonder
why you came home.


00:47:17.324 --> 00:47:20.452
- Mark collected
all of our writings,


00:47:20.493 --> 00:47:23.394
pieces that all of us had done
about what had gone wrong


00:47:23.430 --> 00:47:25.455
in the ways that we studied
previous drugs


00:47:25.498 --> 00:47:27.966
and what we were
already doing wrong


00:47:28.001 --> 00:47:29.229
in terms of trying
to figure out


00:47:29.269 --> 00:47:30.896
whether the protease inhibitors
worked,


00:47:30.937 --> 00:47:32.404
and we published it
as this big report


00:47:32.439 --> 00:47:34.373
and started passing them out
everywhere we went.


00:47:34.407 --> 00:47:35.806
Let's do this study.


00:47:35.842 --> 00:47:37.241
- They elevated themselves


00:47:37.277 --> 00:47:40.804
by their own self-education
about these things,


00:47:40.847 --> 00:47:42.314
and then it became
very, very clear


00:47:42.349 --> 00:47:44.317
that you weren't gonna
mess with these people,


00:47:44.351 --> 00:47:47.479
because they knew exactly
what you were talking about,


00:47:47.520 --> 00:47:49.988
and they knew exactly
what they were talking about.


00:47:50.023 --> 00:47:52.321
- Activists created a system


00:47:52.359 --> 00:47:53.656
that was able to do
everything


00:47:53.693 --> 00:47:55.991
faster, better, cheaper,


00:47:56.029 --> 00:47:59.294
more ethically,
and more effectively.


00:47:59.332 --> 00:48:01.163
- They forced people
to put together


00:48:01.201 --> 00:48:02.930
the right clinical trials,


00:48:02.969 --> 00:48:05.164
where you had the patient
in mind,


00:48:05.205 --> 00:48:07.503
and you weren't
cutting corners.


00:48:07.540 --> 00:48:09.007
And, of course,
the big breakthrough


00:48:09.042 --> 00:48:10.441
was combination therapy,


00:48:10.477 --> 00:48:13.674
'cause monotherapy
was clearly not the way to go.


00:48:13.713 --> 00:48:15.180
- So let's go back
into patients,


00:48:15.215 --> 00:48:16.512
and let's go back
into patients


00:48:16.549 --> 00:48:19.040
with Crixivan
plus AZT plus 3TC.


00:48:19.085 --> 00:48:21.019
- The activists
proposed a study design;


00:48:21.054 --> 00:48:22.180
industry used it.


00:48:22.222 --> 00:48:24.349
It got the drug approved
in six months.


00:48:24.391 --> 00:48:26.188
- So I was at a meeting
in Washington,


00:48:26.226 --> 00:48:28.854
and I stood up there,


00:48:28.895 --> 00:48:32.854
and I showed for the first time
the data in the study.


00:48:32.899 --> 00:48:34.526
- The data
goes up on the screen,


00:48:34.567 --> 00:48:37.695
and everyone gasps,
and cameras start to click.


00:48:37.737 --> 00:48:40.137
And just--it's a realization
that this is--


00:48:40.173 --> 00:48:43.040
this is a really great drug,
and it--it could work.


00:48:43.076 --> 00:48:45.203
- I remember sitting there
in a hotel room


00:48:45.245 --> 00:48:46.371
in Washington, D.C.,


00:48:46.413 --> 00:48:48.881
our hotel banquet room,


00:48:48.915 --> 00:48:51.213
and just crying.


00:48:51.251 --> 00:48:54.049
It was like, "We did it.
We did something."


00:48:54.087 --> 00:48:55.714
- It was too much to take in
at that point.


00:48:55.755 --> 00:48:56.847
It wasn't until we started


00:48:56.890 --> 00:48:58.881
putting the drugs
in our bodies


00:48:58.925 --> 00:49:01.553
and we all went home and...


00:49:01.594 --> 00:49:04.222
started--
went straight on that regimen


00:49:04.264 --> 00:49:06.391
that had been on that slide,


00:49:06.433 --> 00:49:10.733
including Crixivan
and two nucleoside analogues.


00:49:10.770 --> 00:49:12.567
And sure enough,


00:49:12.605 --> 00:49:15.073
it happened in us
within 30 days,


00:49:15.108 --> 00:49:16.234
all of us.


00:49:16.276 --> 00:49:18.471
Undetectable, undetectable,
undetectable.


00:49:18.511 --> 00:49:22.504
- The dying was stopping
with triple-drug combination.


00:49:22.549 --> 00:49:25.074
And if you needed
your clinical trial,


00:49:25.118 --> 00:49:26.745
you could just
go to these hospitals


00:49:26.786 --> 00:49:28.253
that were not filling up


00:49:28.288 --> 00:49:31.086
the way they were filling up
with people with HIV.


00:49:31.124 --> 00:49:33.422
- You would see
their Kaposi's sarcoma lesions


00:49:33.460 --> 00:49:37.760
that had been bright
and red and big


00:49:37.797 --> 00:49:39.765
melting back into their skin.


00:49:39.799 --> 00:49:41.460
- They were calling it
the Lazarus effect.


00:49:41.501 --> 00:49:44.095
People who were deathly ill
would get put on this drug,


00:49:44.137 --> 00:49:46.264
and all of a sudden,
they're working again.


00:49:46.306 --> 00:49:48.570
That was a phenomenal feeling.


00:49:48.608 --> 00:49:49.939
- It worked.


00:49:49.976 --> 00:49:52.638
You know,
we did something remarkable.


00:49:52.679 --> 00:49:54.112
- So that breakthrough,
you know,


00:49:54.147 --> 00:49:58.948
that we thought was gonna happen
in '88 or '89


00:49:58.985 --> 00:50:01.112
if we just worked
fast enough,


00:50:01.154 --> 00:50:02.815
you know, it did happen.


00:50:06.326 --> 00:50:09.159
But not until '96,
and so...


00:50:10.830 --> 00:50:12.627
You know,
a lot of people died.


00:50:12.665 --> 00:50:16.465
Maybe if Reagan had started
putting money into AIDS


00:50:16.503 --> 00:50:18.334
a little earlier...


00:50:26.846 --> 00:50:28.643
They wouldn't all be dead.


00:50:31.684 --> 00:50:33.777
- I feel very fortunate.


00:50:33.820 --> 00:50:38.484
And there's probably a lot
of complicated reasons why,


00:50:38.525 --> 00:50:40.493
but I still
find it very difficult


00:50:40.527 --> 00:50:41.994
to plan for the future


00:50:42.028 --> 00:50:44.326
and/or accept that


00:50:44.364 --> 00:50:48.767
I will have a long life,


00:50:48.801 --> 00:50:52.430
which is unfortunate,
because I've had a long life,


00:50:52.472 --> 00:50:57.000
and I've been living with AIDS
for 20 years.


00:50:57.043 --> 00:51:02.174
But it's hard for me
to relax into life.


00:51:02.215 --> 00:51:05.184
- I know lots of us
went through


00:51:05.218 --> 00:51:08.847
really difficult times
after,


00:51:08.888 --> 00:51:11.015
um, trying to figure out,


00:51:11.057 --> 00:51:13.184
"Well, what do I do now?"


00:51:13.226 --> 00:51:14.659
You know?


00:51:14.694 --> 00:51:17.322
Not just because I didn't think
I had a future and now I do,


00:51:17.363 --> 00:51:21.026
so I have to make some plans,
but...


00:51:21.067 --> 00:51:24.696
How do I do something else
that is as...


00:51:26.406 --> 00:51:28.203
I mean, it's a weird word,


00:51:28.241 --> 00:51:32.541
but as fulfilling
as that work has been.


00:51:32.579 --> 00:51:37.539
- To be that threatened


00:51:37.584 --> 00:51:42.715
with extinction, um...


00:51:42.755 --> 00:51:48.557
And to not lay down, um...


00:51:48.595 --> 00:51:51.393
But instead to stand up


00:51:51.431 --> 00:51:55.231
and to fight back.


00:51:55.268 --> 00:51:57.236
The way we did it,


00:51:57.270 --> 00:52:01.229
the way we took care
of ourselves and each other,


00:52:01.274 --> 00:52:04.573
the goodness that we showed,


00:52:04.611 --> 00:52:08.240
the humanity
that we showed the world


00:52:08.281 --> 00:52:10.579
is just mind-boggling,


00:52:10.617 --> 00:52:13.415
just incredible.


00:52:13.453 --> 00:52:14.477
Fight AIDS!


00:52:14.520 --> 00:52:16.715
Act up!
Fight back!


00:52:16.756 --> 00:52:17.916
Fight AIDS!


00:52:17.957 --> 00:52:19.584
- Every single drug
that's out there


00:52:19.626 --> 00:52:22.424
is because of ACT UP,
I am convinced.


00:52:22.462 --> 00:52:24.760
We had the brainpower,
and we had the street power.


00:52:24.797 --> 00:52:26.765
We had the good cops
and the bad cops.


00:52:26.799 --> 00:52:28.596
The government
didn't get us the drugs.


00:52:28.635 --> 00:52:30.762
No one else got us the drugs.


00:52:30.803 --> 00:52:34.603
We, ACT UP,
got those drugs out there.


00:52:34.641 --> 00:52:37.439
It is the proudest achievement


00:52:37.477 --> 00:52:39.775
that the gay population
of this world


00:52:39.812 --> 00:52:41.370
can ever claim.


00:52:41.414 --> 00:52:43.609
- We could do it
because we could deliver


00:52:43.650 --> 00:52:46.744
hundreds and sometimes thousands
of bodies.


00:52:46.786 --> 00:52:50.278
We had people with AIDS putting
their bodies on the line,


00:52:50.323 --> 00:52:51.620
flopping out in the streets,


00:52:51.658 --> 00:52:53.922
saying,
"Fine, this is my body.


00:52:53.960 --> 00:52:55.291
Take me away."


00:52:55.328 --> 00:52:56.522
- I drew the line there.


00:52:58.498 --> 00:52:59.965
I didn't want
to get arrested.


00:53:02.168 --> 00:53:03.999
That far, I wouldn't go.


00:53:15.014 --> 00:53:18.313
- ♪ Keeping up ♪


00:53:18.351 --> 00:53:22.549
♪ Keeping up with the feeling,
oh, yeah ♪


00:53:22.588 --> 00:53:25.148
♪ Getting to know
what you like ♪


00:53:25.191 --> 00:53:27.318
♪ And what you love ♪


00:53:27.360 --> 00:53:29.487
- I'd like to close
with words written


00:53:29.529 --> 00:53:32.157
by fellow AIDS activist
Vito Russo:


00:53:32.198 --> 00:53:36.999
"When future generations ask
what we did in the war,


00:53:37.036 --> 00:53:38.731
"we have to be able
to tell them


00:53:38.771 --> 00:53:40.898
"that we were out here
fighting,


00:53:40.940 --> 00:53:42.999
"and we have to leave
a legacy


00:53:43.042 --> 00:53:46.671
"to the generations of people
who will come after us.


00:53:46.713 --> 00:53:51.173
"Remember that someday
the AIDS crisis will be over.


00:53:51.217 --> 00:53:53.845
"And when that day
has come and gone,


00:53:53.886 --> 00:53:56.684
"there will be a people
alive on this Earth,


00:53:56.723 --> 00:53:58.691
"gay people
and straight people,


00:53:58.725 --> 00:54:00.352
"black people
and white people,


00:54:00.393 --> 00:54:01.690
"men and women,


00:54:01.728 --> 00:54:03.525
"who will hear the story


00:54:03.563 --> 00:54:06.862
"that once there was
a terrible disease,


00:54:06.899 --> 00:54:10.198
"and that a brave group
of people stood up and fought--


00:54:10.236 --> 00:54:12.033
"and in some cases died--


00:54:12.071 --> 00:54:15.040
so that others might live
and be free."


